    "feedSummary": "Hélène Landemore, Professor of Political Science at Yale University and a fellow at Oxford’s Institute for Ethics in AI, joins Kevin Frazier, director of the AI Innovation and Law Program at the University of Texas School of Law and a Senior Editor at Lawfare, to examine who should decide what AI is for. Starting with Landemore’s recent Noema essay, the pair discuss why decisions about AI’s purpose, limits, and governance should not be left solely to elected officials, technical experts, and the companies building the systems. Landemore explains some key ideas--covering the fundamentals of deliberative democracy, sortition, and citizens’ assemblies. They then explore her proposal for global public consultation and deliberation. The duo conclude with hard questions about representation, expertise, and platform power.",
    "doomIndexInputSummary": "Scaling Laws: Public Participation in AI Governance With Hélène Landemore. Hélène Landemore, Professor of Political Science at Yale University and a fellow at Oxford’s Institute for Ethics in AI, joins Kevin Frazier, director of the AI Innovation and Law Program at the University of Texas School of Law and a Senior Editor at Lawfare, to examine who should decide what AI is for. Starting with Landemore’s recent Noema essay, the pair discuss why decisions about AI’s purpose, limits, and governance should not be left solely to elected officials, technical experts, and the companies building the systems. Landemore explains some key ideas--covering the fundamentals of deliberative democracy, sortition, and citizens’ assemblies. They then explore her proposal for global public consultation and deliberation. The duo conclude with hard questions about representation, expertise, and platform power."
